<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0, viewport-fit=cover" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#FFFFFF" />
    <base href="/" />
    @* No static <title> here: every page supplies its own through <PageTitle>, and with
       prerendering the HeadOutlet emits a real <title> element into this head. *@
    <meta name="description" content="bit BbScanner (Bit.BbScanner) - a dependency-free barcode scanner for Blazor: QR Code, Data Matrix, Code 128, Code 39, EAN-13, EAN-8, UPC-A, UPC-E and ITF from the camera or from still images, with the decoding written in C#." />

    @* Link previews: the card a chat client or a social site unfurls is the first impression a
       shared link makes. *@
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Bit.BbScanner" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="BbScanner - barcode scanning for Blazor, decoded in C#" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="QR Code, Data Matrix, Code 128, Code 39, EAN, UPC and ITF from the camera or from still images - no barcode SDK, no JavaScript scanner library." />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />

    <ResourcePreloader />
    @* The site kit (app.css, theme.js, site.js - shared by every Utilities demo), then this demo's
       own sample styles. None of them is needed by the scanner itself.

       The scanner stylesheet (_content/Bit.BbScanner/bit-bbscanner.css) is not linked here:
       Bit.BbScanner's JS initializer adds it to the page by itself. Its defaults sit in a :where()
       block, so the brand tint demo.css gives the scanner wins whichever file loads last. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["demo.css"]" />
    @* Scoped-CSS bundle: includes the client project's *.razor.css files as well. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["Bit.BbScanner.Demo.Server.styles.css"]" />
    @* Synchronous on purpose: applies the light/dark theme before first paint. *@
    <script src="@Assets["theme.js"]"></script>
    <ImportMap />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="favicon.png" />
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
</head>

@* data-enhance-nav="false": a link followed before the WebAssembly runtime has started is a plain
   page load rather than an enhanced one. Enhanced navigation fetches and merges the next page while
   the runtime is still starting, and the router the runtime then attaches can render the address
   it read before the merge - the URL says /getting-started and the page is the landing page. Once the
   app is interactive its router handles every link itself and this attribute is not consulted. *@
<body data-enhance-nav="false">
    @* InteractiveWebAssembly rather than Auto or Server: BbScanner reads camera frames through
       [JSImport] memory views, which exist only on WebAssembly - under Server rendering it reports
       NotSupported. The pages are still prerendered, so the first response carries them. *@
    <Routes @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
    <div id="blazor-error-ui" data-nosnippet>
        An unhandled error has occurred.
        <a href="." class="reload">Reload</a>
        <span class="dismiss">&#128473;</span>
    </div>
    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
    <script src="@Assets["site.js"]"></script>
</body>

</html>
